{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner answers the four questions of authority revision 25 (a1d18e51, builder's request 693ce95a, invitation b7c8f475). (1) The single-controller exception takes effect for an agent's change when the one controller is the controller of an agent among the manifest's authors, as revision 24's third condition read: 'among the manifest's authors, or the controller of an agent among them'. My words 'who is the author' were shorthand for that condition, not a narrowing of it. The reason is the case the demo exists for: one person directing agents must be able to change the repository's rules without writing a commit by hand, and the controller stands behind work done under their grant. Keep the clause in judgeExtents; the text follows revision 24 here. (2) A link that leaves the tree or cannot be resolved is refused as a change to the rules extent: never met, by nobody's review. The way forward is to change the link, as decision dc487bcf states. (3) Yes: a changed path whose name is not well-formed UTF-8 or holds a control byte is not met, with rules named, until IA says how a reader carries one. Fail closed. (4) Yes: a repository has the first definition's three extents before its first publish, so no change is ever judged by one bar. These four go into the revision as decided; the checker's review of revision 25 may proceed against them."}